---
import Slide from '@decks/templates/Slide.astro';

// One rejection, not two. The dim was explored as a presence treatment
// and the lock as a concurrency rule, but on a slide they are the same
// idea: the dim IS how a locked row says it cannot be typed in. Showing
// them apart made the deck argue twice about one direction.
//
// So the bench runs both at once, mark=floatdim with lock=locked, and
// the slide carries both reasons.
//
// Drive it: try to type in the row somebody else is holding.
//
// The design reason: a lock is not a design, it is scaffolding. It is
// what you build when you have given up on merging, and it teaches a
// habit that has to be untrained the moment real collaborative editing
// lands.
//
// The measured reason: the dim that announces the lock is 2.67:1 against
// the 4.5:1 required, and no opacity value saves it, because the
// treatment works by weakening the text and the text is what has to stay
// readable. The affordance fails on its own terms even if the rule were
// right.
//
// solo=1 starts somebody typing immediately. The old scheduler waited a
// random two to five seconds, so the slide showed an ordinary list and
// the rejection was invisible.
---

<Slide subtitle="Lock the row" badge="Rejected">
	<iframe
		src="/decks/2026-checklist-component/prototypes/edit-presence-bench?embed=1&scale=1.6&solo=1&mark=floatdim&lock=locked&fit=center"
		title="A locked row, dimmed to say so, rejected"
		class="live-embed m-0! block h-[800px] w-[1704px] max-h-none! max-w-none! border-0"
	></iframe>
</Slide>
